Bash is a commnad language interpreter that provides an environemnt in which users can execute commands and run applications. 


Enviornmental Setup

Accessing the BASH shell: 

  Kali & MacOS use Z Shell by default, so when you open a new terminal window, you'll have to enter exec bash to switch to a bash shell before you run commands. 

  If you want to change your default shell to bash so you don't have to manually switch shells, you can use the chsh -s /bin/bash command. 

  If you're running Windows, you can use the Windows subsystem for Linux(WSL), which lets you run Linux distributions and access a bash environment. 

  An alternative to WSL is Cygwin, which emulates a Linux environmentby providing a colleciton of Linux utilites and system-call functionalities. 

Installing a Text Editor 

  To start writing scripts, you'll need a text editor, preferably one with handy features such as syntax highlighting built in. 

  You can choose between terminal-based editors or graphical user interface-based text editors. 

    Terminal-based text editors: vi or GNU nano

    Graphical text editor: Sublime text. 

Exploring the Shell

  Enter the following command to verify that bash is available on your system: 

    $ bash --version

  Checking Environment Variables

    When running in terminal, bash loads a set of environment variables with every new session that gets invoked. 

    To see the list of environment variables set by bash, run the env command directly from the shell

    $ env

    You can read individual environment variables by using the echo command, which writes text to the terminal. 

    For example, to print the default shell for the user, use the SHELL environment variable preceded by a dollar sign. ($) and surrounded by curly brackets ({}). 

    $ echo ${shell}

    Some default environment variables available: 

    BASH_VERSION: The bash version running

    BASHPID: The process identifier (PID) of the current bash process

    GROUPS: A list of groups the running user is a member of

    HOSTNAME: The name of the host

    OSTYPE: The type of operating system

    PWD: The current working directory 

    RANDOM: A random number from 0 to 32767

    UID: The user ID (UID) of the current user

    SHELL: The full pathname to the shell

    $ echo {random}

    $ echo {UID}

    $ echo {OSTYPE} (Page 4) 

    These commands generate a random number, output the current user's ID, and display the operating system type, respectively. 

Running Linux Commands

  You can insert the man(manual commmand) before any Linux command to open a terminal-based guide that explains the command's use and options. 

    $ man ls 

    Linux commands can accept many types of input on  the command line. 

    Arguements are passed on the command line by using either short-form or long-form argument syntax, depending on the cmmand in use. 
    Short-form syntax uses a single dash (-) followed by one or more characters. 

    $ ls -1

    Some commands let you supply multiple arguments by joining them together or listing them separately: 

    $ ls -la
    $ ls -l -a 

    Note that some commands may throw errors if you attempt to join two arguments with a single dash. 

    Some command options may allow you to use long-form argument syntax, such as the --helpcommand to list the available options. 
    Long-form argument syntax is prepended by the double dash symbol (--).

    $ ls --help

    Sometimes the command argument supports both short- and long-form argument syntax for convenience. 
    However, you could also pass the argement --all, and the outcome would be identical. 

    ls -a
    ls --all. (Page 5) 

